##################################################################################
# THREAD B stage 2 -- relation height vs span: the finiteness dichotomy
##################################################################################

==================================================================================
Q1 -- first minimum h(E) small does NOT bound span (one free stranger)
==================================================================================
  Family {0,1,2,...,k-2, N}: the bounded part {0..k-2} has h=1 (e.g. 1+1-2=0),
  but N is a FREE large offset => span unbounded with h(E)=1.  Confirm:
   k      N  h(E)   span    measS7
   8     10     1     10    0.1905
   8     50     1     50    0.1976
   8    200     1    200    0.1940
   8   1000     1   1000    0.1968
  => first-minimum smallness alone gives an INFINITE family. Need a stronger notion.

==================================================================================
Q2 -- the LAST minimum / FULL low-height lattice DOES bound span
==================================================================================
  If the relation lattice Lambda(E) is GENERATED by vectors of height <= H (i.e.
  the rank-(d-1) lattice has all successive minima <= H), then the speeds satisfy
  d-1 independent low-height relations, pinning the speed RATIOS to a bounded set.
  Test: for primitive E, if rank of {||n||<=H relations} = d-1, span is bounded by f(H,k).

  shape                               d rank(H=2)  d-1   span full-lowH?
  consec8 {0..7}                      7         6    6      7       True
  {0,1,2,3,4,5,6,8}                   7         6    6      8       True
  {0,1,2,...,6,40} stranger           7         6    6     40       True
  {0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14} AP*2           7         6    6     14       True
  {0,1,3,7,12,20,30,44} Sidon         7         6    6     44       True
  {0,3,6,9,12,15,18,21} AP*3          7         6    6     21       True
  {0,1,2,4,8,16,32,64} geom           7         6    6     64       True

  => 'full-low-height' (rank of height<=H relations = d-1) is the FINITENESS criterion.
     These are the sub-AP / Freiman-structured shapes.  Strangers/Sidon are NOT full-low-H.

==================================================================================
Q3 -- EXPLICIT span bound for full-low-height (sub-AP) primitive shapes
==================================================================================
  CLAIM: if the relation lattice of the nonzero speeds is generated by height<=H
  vectors (full rank d-1), then primitivity forces span <= S(d,H).  We MEASURE the
  largest span achievable by a primitive full-low-height shape, by enumeration.

  Enumerate primitive E (0 in E, |E|=k) with span<=SP and check full-low-height at H;
  report the MAX span among full-low-height ones (should saturate => finite family).
  k=8 H=1: #full-low-H primitive with span<=14: 3313; MAX span among them = 14  e.g. [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 14]
